import{_t as e,it as t,n,rt as r}from"./chunks/framework.B5d4da8a.js";var i=JSON.parse('{"title":"Module Resolution — Path-Based Imports + .handl.toml Path Dependencies — design detail","description":"","frontmatter":{"title":"Module Resolution — Path-Based Imports + `.handl.toml` Path Dependencies — design detail","type":"design","date":"2026-09-08T00:00:00.000Z","updated":"2026-10-29T00:00:00.000Z","refs":["ADR-0096"]},"headers":[],"relativePath":"handl/project/design/0096-module-resolution.md","filePath":"vendored/handl/project/design/0096-module-resolution.md"}'),a={name:`handl/project/design/0096-module-resolution.md`};function o(n,i,a,o,s,c){return e(),r(`div`,null,[...i[0]||=[t(`<h1 id="module-resolution-—-path-based-imports-handl-toml-path-dependencies-—-design-detail" tabindex="-1">Module Resolution — Path-Based Imports + <code>.handl.toml</code> Path Dependencies — design detail <a class="header-anchor" href="#module-resolution-—-path-based-imports-handl-toml-path-dependencies-—-design-detail" aria-label="Permalink to “Module Resolution — Path-Based Imports + .handl.toml Path Dependencies — design detail”">​</a></h1><p>Moved verbatim from ADR-0096 (ADR-0161 size budget). The ADR carries the rules; this page carries the context, tables, phases and trade-offs.</p><h2 id="context" tabindex="-1">Context <a class="header-anchor" href="#context" aria-label="Permalink to “Context”">​</a></h2><p>The <code>import</code> op exists in all three eval engines and <code>handl-runtime</code> owns a <code>ModuleLoader</code> (<code>load_module</code>/<code>resolve_import</code>), but two pieces were missing: a stated resolution policy for the <code>import &quot;path&quot;</code> specifier, and a way for a project to name external packages (<code>.handl.toml</code> had no <code>[dependencies]</code> section). ADR-0010 sketches the long-term package-manager design (content-addressed graph stores, registries, lockfiles) — it is a large design not needed to unblock local development.</p><h2 id="decision" tabindex="-1">Decision <a class="header-anchor" href="#decision" aria-label="Permalink to “Decision”">​</a></h2><p>Module resolution is <strong>path-based</strong>, with a dependency table as the only indirection. Given <code>import &quot;&lt;spec&gt;&quot;</code>:</p><ol><li><strong>Dependency specifier.</strong> If the first <code>/</code>-separated segment of <code>&lt;spec&gt;</code> names an entry in the project&#39;s <code>.handl.toml</code> <code>[dependencies]</code> table, the remainder resolves inside that package&#39;s directory: <code>import &quot;arrows/fn/map.hnl&quot;</code> → <code>&lt;deps[&quot;arrows&quot;]&gt;/fn/map.hnl</code>. Dependency names shadow same-named directories relative to the module root.</li><li><strong>Relative path.</strong> Otherwise <code>&lt;spec&gt;</code> resolves against the directory of the module currently being loaded (nested imports), falling back to the configured module root, then the process working directory.</li></ol><p><code>[dependencies]</code> maps an import name to a package directory, in either form:</p><div class="language-toml"><button title="Copy code" data-copied="Copied" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">toml</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes handl-dark github-light" style="--shiki-dark:#ffffff;--shiki-light:#24292e;--shiki-dark-bg:#2a2a2a;--shiki-light-bg:#fff;" tabindex="0" dir="ltr"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-dark:#888888;--shiki-light:#24292E;">[</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#FFFFFF;--shiki-light:#6F42C1;">dependencies</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#888888;--shiki-light:#24292E;">]</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-dark:#FFFFFF;--shiki-light:#24292E;">arrows </span><span style="--shiki-dark:#888888;--shiki-light:#24292E;">=</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#888888;--shiki-light:#032F62;"> &quot;</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#FFFFFF;--shiki-light:#032F62;">vendor/arrows</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#888888;--shiki-light:#032F62;">&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#888888;--shiki-dark-font-style:italic;--shiki-light:#62687b;--shiki-light-font-style:inherit;">          #</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#BBBBBB;--shiki-dark-font-style:italic;--shiki-light:#62687b;--shiki-light-font-style:inherit;"> bare path</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-dark:#FFFFFF;--shiki-light:#24292E;">utils  </span><span style="--shiki-dark:#888888;--shiki-light:#24292E;">=</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#888888;--shiki-light:#24292E;"> {</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#FFFFFF;--shiki-light:#24292E;"> path </span><span style="--shiki-dark:#888888;--shiki-light:#24292E;">=</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#888888;--shiki-light:#032F62;"> &quot;</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#FFFFFF;--shiki-light:#032F62;">../shared</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#888888;--shiki-light:#032F62;">&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#888888;--shiki-light:#24292E;"> }</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#888888;--shiki-dark-font-style:italic;--shiki-light:#62687b;--shiki-light-font-style:inherit;">   #</span><span style="--shiki-dark:#BBBBBB;--shiki-dark-font-style:italic;--shiki-light:#62687b;--shiki-light-font-style:inherit;"> table form (room for future specifiers)</span></span></code></pre></div><p>Paths are relative to the manifest directory (absolute paths honored as-is). The project manifest is discovered by walking up from the entry file&#39;s directory (<code>Kit::discover</code> / <code>Kit::discover_with_eval</code>); the manifest&#39;s directory becomes the module root. With no manifest, the entry directory is the module root and there are no dependencies.</p><p>Only <strong>vendored / path dependencies</strong> are supported — no registry, no network, no version resolution. Registry and content-addressed stores remain the ADR-0010 design and can layer a <code>spec</code>-to-directory resolution step in front of this mechanism without changing import semantics.</p><h3 id="arrow-value-sealing" tabindex="-1">Arrow-value sealing <a class="header-anchor" href="#arrow-value-sealing" aria-label="Permalink to “Arrow-value sealing”">​</a></h3><p>Exported arrow values carry <code>body</code> indices into the <em>module&#39;s</em> AST. Before bindings cross into the importer&#39;s scope, <code>EvalEngine::seal_module_value</code> (a defaulted trait hook — identity for engines whose values carry no ambient-eval references) lets the tree-walk engine register the module&#39;s eval frame (<code>register_home_frame</code>) and stamp un-homed arrows with <code>__home__</code>, the same provenance mechanism <code>HandlFunction</code> already uses.</p><h2 id="unified-import-model-amendment" tabindex="-1">Unified import model (amendment) <a class="header-anchor" href="#unified-import-model-amendment" aria-label="Permalink to “Unified import model (amendment)”">​</a></h2><p>There are two specifier domains sharing one statement family (<code>stmt.import</code>):</p><h3 id="canonical-surface-forms" tabindex="-1">Canonical surface forms <a class="header-anchor" href="#canonical-surface-forms" aria-label="Permalink to “Canonical surface forms”">​</a></h3><p>All of these parse under the canonical Pratt grammar and lower to a <code>stmt.import</code> node carrying <code>payload.path</code>, <code>payload.names</code> (<code>&quot;*&quot;</code> or a single export name), and <code>payload.alias</code>:</p><div class="language-handl"><button title="Copy code" data-copied="Copied" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">handl</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes handl-dark github-light" style="--shiki-dark:#ffffff;--shiki-light:#24292e;--shiki-dark-bg:#2a2a2a;--shiki-light-bg:#fff;" tabindex="0" dir="ltr"><code><span class="line"><span>import &quot;&lt;spec&gt;&quot;                       -- splice all exports into scope</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>from &quot;&lt;spec&gt;&quot; import *                -- same, explicit</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>from &quot;&lt;spec&gt;&quot; import * as &lt;alias&gt;     -- bind the module record to &lt;alias&gt;</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>from &quot;&lt;spec&gt;&quot; import &lt;name&gt;           -- bind &lt;name&gt; to the module&#39;s export</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>from &quot;&lt;spec&gt;&quot; import &lt;name&gt; as &lt;alias&gt; -- bind &lt;alias&gt; to the export</span></span></code></pre></div><p>The <code>as</code> tail is gated on the <code>as</code> keyword matching (<code>TokenStep::requires_prev_match</code>) so a bare <code>from &quot;p&quot; import name</code> followed by another statement never swallows that statement&#39;s leading identifier.</p><p>For a <strong>path</strong> <code>&lt;spec&gt;</code> (anything that resolves to a file per the Decision rules — <code>[dependencies]</code> first segment, module dir, module root, CWD) the op is executable in all three eval engines: <code>prim_import</code> projects a named export (erroring on a missing name), binds the module record under <code>* as</code>, or splices exports. Named imports (<code>from &quot;p&quot; import name</code>) are the canonical replacement for the old <code>import name from &quot;p&quot;</code> order.</p><h3 id="module-name-imports" tabindex="-1">Module-name imports <a class="header-anchor" href="#module-name-imports" aria-label="Permalink to “Module-name imports”">​</a></h3><p>A <code>&lt;spec&gt;</code> that is a <strong>bare host-module name</strong> — a single <code>[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_-]*</code> segment that does not resolve to a file — denotes a module on the host module chain (<code>str</code>, <code>fn</code>, <code>dedent</code>, ...), not a path. Resolution:</p><ul><li>In <code>.hnl</code> sources loaded as <code>HandlModule</code>s, the declarations are already semantic: <code>analyze_source</code> parses them and <code>HandlModule::with_resolver</code> auto-wires the named modules as bridge-runtime dependencies.</li><li>In whole-program hosts (<code>hnl run</code>, <code>hnl test</code>) the kit&#39;s module chain already supplies the bindings (qualified <code>str.trim</code> and bare <code>reverse</code> both resolve), so the declaration lines are <strong>decorative and stripped textually</strong> — <code>host-cli::strip_import_declarations</code> drops <code>from</code> lines whose specifier does not resolve to a file plus all <code>import &lt;name&gt; ...</code> lines; the test runner skips them unconditionally. (<code>hnl eval</code> evaluates a single expression, where in-expression <code>stmt.import</code> nodes execute normally.)</li><li>In file-loaded modules, <code>ModuleLoader::strip_module_decl_lines</code> applies the same rule before lexing, so a <code>.hnl</code> module file may carry <code>from &quot;str&quot; import * as str</code> headers without failing as a missing file.</li></ul><p><strong>Deferred:</strong> a <code>resolve_import</code> fallback that resolves bare names against the module registry (returning a module-namespace value) is future work, pending <code>loader.hnl</code> becoming the installed default transform (the <code>HandlLoader</code>/<code>install_loader</code> seam). The value model does not currently expose a host module as a first-class record, so declarations suffice.</p><p><em>Loader status update:</em> the parsed-AST cache in <code>HandlFunction::call</code> (thread-local <code>AST_CACHE</code> + <code>EvalEngine::eval_shared</code> / <code>Runtime::eval_shared_with_globals</code>) removed the per-call re-parse that originally blocked the default install — measured ~7× faster on <code>loader.strip_decls</code> over <code>dedent.hnl</code> and ~19× on warm <code>str.*</code> calls. The remaining blocker is the interpreted <code>str.*</code>/<code>char.*</code> body eval over every input character (~43s release for a 22k-char transform), so <code>loader.hnl</code> stays opt-in; see AGENTS.md &quot;Loader self-hosting&quot; for the measured numbers and the <code>plugin-lib-handl/examples/loader_bench.rs</code> harness.</p><h3 id="static-checking" tabindex="-1">Static checking <a class="header-anchor" href="#static-checking" aria-label="Permalink to “Static checking”">​</a></h3><p><code>hnl typecheck</code> scopes the bound names of every <code>stmt.import</code> — the child <code>var</code> (<code>import name</code>), <code>payload.alias</code>, and <code>payload.names</code> — both at module level and inside <code>do { ... }</code> blocks, where statements bind sequentially (an <code>assign</code> target or import is in scope for following siblings). <code>from &quot;&lt;bare-name&gt;&quot; import *</code> scopes the module name itself so <code>str.trim</code> is not an unbound wire. Splice forms with a path specifier (<code>import &quot;p&quot;</code>, <code>from &quot;p&quot; import *</code>) cannot be statically enumerated; names they bind are still flagged by the bootstrap checker — use the <code>* as</code> or named form for statically-checked code.</p><h3 id="arrow-module-dialect" tabindex="-1">Arrow-module dialect <a class="header-anchor" href="#arrow-module-dialect" aria-label="Permalink to “Arrow-module dialect”">​</a></h3><p><code>plugin-ext-refinement</code> arrow modules keep their own surface syntax — <code>import &lt;name&gt;[, &lt;name&gt;...] from &quot;path&quot;</code>, <code>import &quot;path&quot; as alias</code>, <code>export ... from</code> — scanned by <code>arrow_parser</code>&#39;s line scanner before arrow expressions are parsed. This is <strong>dialect-fixed</strong>, not migrated: the whole file format (arrow graphs, <code>rec { ... } ( ... )</code>, <code>~&gt;</code>) is the dialect; the only in-tree uses are <code>handl-packages/collection/sort.hnl</code> and the <code>research/arrow-calculus-kernel-poc</code> corpus, exercised by refinement tests. Note the canonical Pratt parser accepts <code>import x from &quot;p&quot;</code> as <code>import (x)</code> and silently drops the trailing <code>from &quot;p&quot;</code> tokens (the parser does not check cursor exhaustion) — the bootstrap typechecker&#39;s decl collection reads the var child, which coincidentally matches the dialect&#39;s bound name, so <code>hnl typecheck</code>/<code>lint</code> accept such lines while <code>hnl run</code> strips them.</p><h2 id="consequences" tabindex="-1">Consequences <a class="header-anchor" href="#consequences" aria-label="Permalink to “Consequences”">​</a></h2><ul><li><code>import &quot;arrows/fn/map.hnl&quot;</code> works end-to-end via <code>hnl run</code>/<code>hnl eval</code> and <code>Kit::discover</code>; covered by <code>kit-canonical/tests/import_export.rs</code>, which also covers <code>from &quot;p&quot; import *</code> / <code>* as</code> / <code>name</code> / <code>name as</code> forms and file-loaded modules carrying host-module headers.</li><li>A module file is a single top-level statement (Pratt convention); its export is the root node&#39;s binding target (<code>x :: expr</code> → <code>x</code>). Multi- statement modules use a <code>do { ... }</code> root, which exports nothing.</li><li><code>from &quot;&lt;name&gt;&quot; import</code> forms still name <em>modules</em> resolved via the <code>HandlModule</code>/<code>ModuleResolver</code> seam, not files — <code>hnl run</code> strips them when the specifier does not resolve to a file. Closing that gap (module-name fallback in <code>resolve_import</code>) is future work.</li><li>The <code>loading</code> cycle-detector and the nested-import directory stack are cleaned up on error paths.</li></ul>`,31)]])}var s=n(a,[[`render`,o]]);export{i as __pageData,s as default};